<title>GERMANY: Rain slows German barley, rapeseed harvesting.</title>
<headline>Rain slows German barley, rapeseed harvesting.</headline>
<dateline>OFFENBACH 1997-07-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>German farmers have had to interrupt the barley harvest because of rainfall this week but they have not reported any quality problems, the DWD meteorological office said in a weekly report.</p>
<p>A DWD source said the weather should remain changeable until the middle of next week, giving barley that had been sown late a chance to ripen fully.</p>
<p>Rainfall was good news for beet and maize while winter rye and wheat were able to tolerate it.</p>
<p>Rapeseed was about to be harvested in the north but field work was likely to be disrupted for at least a few days.</p>
<p>A report from Lower Saxony's farm union said early barley yields looked above average and the crop should be high in quality.</p>
<p>Farmers were storing grain because ex-harvest prices were low.</p>
<p>Grain trader Toepfer on Friday said Germany's grain crop might total 43 million tonnes in 1997 after 42.1 in 1996.</p>
<p>-Vera Eckert, Hamburg newsroom, +49-40-41903275</p>
